WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.880
When I talk to people about doing the Future Authoring program

00:00:02.949 --> 00:00:08.459
they often put it off . And it's not surprising because it's hard. And... But it's more than that

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:13.950
They think well, I don't know how to write. I'm gonna do a bad job. I don't really like assignments.

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:15.660
I'm going to have to do it perfectly.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:18.560
I need to wait till I have enough time.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:24.700
And like one of those is enough to stop you cold, and all five of them - you're just done. And so I tell people

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:27.740
Do it haphazardly,

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:31.140
a tiny bit at a time and badly.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:32.369
Because you can do that.

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:35.900
I tell my students when they're doing their thesis, master's thesis,

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:42.100
write a really bad first draft. And then we have a little conversation about that because they don't think I mean that.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:47.340
Because it sounds like a cliché in some sense. It's not a cliché. It's not a cliche at all.

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:49.720
It means...

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:52.440
You're a terrible writer.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:56.100
But if someone put a gun to your head and said: "You have to have

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:02.740
your hundred page thesis done by next Monday or I'll shoot you, but I don't care how terrible it is".

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:07.580
You would sit down and write it. And the thing is - then you have it! Right, then then you have something

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:13.400
and then you can fix it. You can iterate and fix it - that bad first draft. That's the most valuable thing.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:17.180
And so that's what you need. You need a bad first draft of yourself.

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:24.540
And there's an idea that Jung developed about the Trickster and the Jester the comedian, right,  that the

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:28.520
Trickster is the precursor to the savior. That's one of the things I learned from Jung.

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:37.260
It was just... It's so unlikely you'd never think that. It's so amazing that that might be the case, but the...

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:44.660
satirical and the ironic and the troublemaker that - the comedian - the fool - the fool is the precursor to the savior.

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:49.680
Why? Because you're a fool when you start something new. And so if you're not willing to be a fool

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:53.820
then you'll never start anything new, and if you never start anything new that you won't develop. And so...

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:59.100
The willingness to be a fool is the precursor to transformation. And that's the same as humility.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:05.160
And so if you're gonna write your destiny, you can do a bad first job. You're gonna get smarter as you move forward.

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:09.120
That's the thing. It's that - so something beckons to you. That's what happens here.

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:13.400
Maybe the star that Geppetto wished on was the wrong damn star.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:19.000
But at least it was a star. Right? At least it was in the sky - at least it moved him forward.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:20.480
And so you say in your life...

00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:25.139
Well, something grips you and... and fills you with interest and you think well

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:31.200
Should I do that? And the answer is: "If not that then something."

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.010
What if it's a mistake?

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:34.410
It's a mistake!

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:38.339
Rest assured. What do you know? You're gonna stumble around, right?

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:42.660
And what's gonna happen is this. You're gonna move to you're gonna not stay in stasis.

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:47.100
You're not gonna wander around in circles. And I see people like that. They say well, I never knew what to do.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:49.260
And now I'm 40.

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:51.240
It's like: "That's not so good."

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:58.220
That's not so good. And you might say well... And there is a literature too that suggests that people are a lot more

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:00.940
unhappy when they look back in their lives

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:02.830
about the things they didn't do

00:03:02.830 --> 00:03:09.400
than they are about the mistakes they made while they were doing things. And so that's really worth thinking about too because there's

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:15.660
redemptive mistakes. And a redemptive mistake would be a mistake that you make when you go out and try to do something. And no...

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:20.100
You actually, you think okay. I'm gonna try to do this. And you're not good at it. You make a bunch of mistakes.

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:25.260
It's like: "What's the consequence if you pay attention?" It's you're not quite so stupid anymore.

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:31.160
That's the thing. You've been informed by your... by the results of your errors. And so what happens is...

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:36.660
You... you follow the beacon you follow the light. And... and you're blind! So you don't know where the light is.

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:40.420
It's it's dimly apprehended only and you're afraid to follow it.

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:44.660
But you decide to take some stumbling steps towards it.

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:50.460
And as you take stumbling steps towards it you become illuminated and enlightened and informed.

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:52.120
Because of the nature of your experience.

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:58.420
Because you're pushing yourself beyond where you are and you're going into the country that you have not yet been in. And you learn something.

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:04.340
And so what happens then is that the star moves. You move ten feet towards it. You think no, that's not right.

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:05.380
I didn't get it right.

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:06.150
It isn't there.

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:11.220
It's actually there. And so then you see it somewhere else and you shift yourself slightly.

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:14.580
And you move forward and that's what happens is that

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:22.120
As you continue... as you change, the thing that guides you forward moves.

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:28.800
Right. It's like God in the... in the desert in Egypt. The pillar of light you're following. It's moving!

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:33.740
It's not a permanent thing. You move towards it - it moves away. It guides you forward.

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:38.399
And so you say, well: "Is what I'm aiming at paradise itself?"

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:43.720
And the answer to that is: "No!" Because what do you know? You could you couldn't see paradise if it was right in front of you.

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:45.580
But you might get a glimmer of it.

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:49.400
And so you move towards it and you grow. And then...

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:53.880
The next time you open your eyes, you see a little bit more clearly. And that's what happens.

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:58.500
Is... that just happens over and over. Right? It keeps moving.

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:02.480
And so you move like this.

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:03.800
But the thing that's so cool

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:09.260
is that all those zigs and zags, you say, in each of those zags and zigs is a catastrophe.

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:14.440
I hit a wall, my God, and then I had to die a little bit and I barely got back up. It's a Phoenix

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:22.280
transformation at each... At each turn. And it's painful. But the thing is that even though you've traveled 20

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:29.000
miles, let's say, on that road and you've only moved 3 miles forward - you've moved three miles forward!

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:35.180
Instead of falling backwards, because that's the thing too. If you stand still you fall backwards.

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:41.900
You can not stand still because the world moves away from you if you stand still. And there's no stasis.

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:46.080
There's only backwards. And so if you're not moving...

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:48.960
forwards, then you're moving backwards. And that's...

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:50.900
more...

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:58.320
More of the underlying truth of the Matthew principle: "To those who have everything more will be given.

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:01.420
From those who have nothing everything will be taken."

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:05.480
It's a warning. Do not stay in one place.

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:10.900
Well as you zigzag maybe then...

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:21.280
Maybe the cataclysm of each transformation starts to lessen. There's not so much of you that has to die with every mistake.

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:28.320
And maybe you end up oriented at least reasonably properly. And if you were sensible, that would have been your trip, but it wasn't. Right?

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:30.479
It's that. And perhaps it's a lot worse than that.

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:37.320
Perhaps there's no shortage of backtracking. But it doesn't matter, because as you stumble forward, you...

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:45.220
illuminate and inform yourself. And perhaps that's partly because the world is made of information. And if you...

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:55.440
encounter it and tangle with it, then it informs you. And then you become informed. And then you're information. And then you're ready.

